  um that was surprising to me  um  and then uh you know one last thing is  that if you look at this one L coherence  here which I'm not going to describe  something called a coherence metric  which you may have heard of if you've  heard of LDA if not so I'll talk to me  about it after this the presentation  but you notice if K all the way on the  right there is too small or if it's too  big they're both negative  this is being compared to the Baseline  of a perfectly specified model so what  that tells me is if I get it right  I reach some sort of Maximum there  so that tells me that you know there's  some evidence that says this statistic  might actually be helpful at selecting  the number of topics uh unfortunately  that's a future study so I don't  actually know how to do it yet  um so synthesizing uh uh four key points  and highlighting three uh the biggest  one is we can use simulated data in a  principled way to now actually study  this model and develop some regular  statistical theory on how to use it  um next it looks like Alpha might be  over emphasized in the literature which  is a pretty startling result for the  sake of conservatism I will just say  that there's evidence to revisit uh uh  the conventional wisdom I'm not going to  say that I've overturned it uh and then  finally maybe that coherence metric can  help us choose the number of topics  so  um I've established we can use  simulations in a principled way to study  LDA and so the next two studies that I  did were using those primarily to help  develop in one case a new metric well a  new old metric we'll get into that uh  and then the other a slight modification  of the model in a way that I think is is  helpful to practitioners  um so this first is extending a  well-known statistic for goodness to fit  that's often used in linear regression  uh called the coefficient of  determination or or r squared  um so as a quick review r squared for  linear regression uh it goes between  zero and one it measures the proportion  of variance in your outcome variable  that were exp that was explained by your  model  so if it's one that means that your  model has explained 100 of the variance  in your outcome  uh it is a ratio of a sum of squares  which is the very bottom point there  um that's sort of an important fact to  understand the Insight that I had in  extending this to to work for  multi-dimensional outcomes which is uh  what a topic model models  um so the title here says it all  the traditional sum of squares is uh the  sum of squared euclidean distances take  my word for it but uh if you want to  look at the the formula I can point it  out to you and so I said well if it's  the sum of squared euclidean distances  in one space why can't I just extend it  to squared euclidean distance as an n  space so that's what I did  and this graphic depicts that in two  space right so uh to get a total sum of  squares you would just add up the  squared length of each one of the red  lines on the left there and to get a  residual sum of squares that would just  be the squared length of all the black  lines on the right graphic  so uh most notably this is the exact  same r squared from one ear regression  it's just used generalizing it to  outcomes and N Dimensions like a vector  of word frequencies which is exactly  what topic models model and this isn't  limited to topic models and Theory one  could apply it to deep learning models  of language too  uh the only a note I would give is that  this is now interpreted as the  proportion of variability in your data  explained by your model not variance  because variance is just a very specific  formula that I broke when extending this  to n space  uh so the bottom line is the same r  squared we all know and love all the  same caveats apply but it also means  that we have something that's a little  bit more accessible to an audience that  might be more familiar with linear  regression maybe coming from the social  sciences or from the digital Humanities  but may not be themselves quantitative  experts like statisticians or computer  scientists  okay so last study  um Power along nature of language uh  means that the central limit theorem  which you may or may not have heard from  of in a stats class takes a lot longer  to take effect so the Practical result  of that is that you need much much much  much much larger samples to be able to  get something that is representative of  your population  so for language that means I need a lot  of data  to have something that has decent  statistical power  but that actually provides a theoretical  justification for the use of these  pre-trained language models that have  gotten really popular over the last few  years  so there's been this paradigm shift in  natural language processing previously a  researcher like in most other areas  would take a data set that was theirs  they'd build one model on it  and then try and do some inference or  build a task and that worked you know  okay but it was kind of brittle and now  researchers are starting from these uh  pre-trained language models they're  calling them Foundation models and then  fine-tuning the parameters by  introducing their own data but that  Foundation model can be huge fit on  hundreds of gigabytes of text  they are massive  what I did is I extended LDA to enable  fine-tuning of these pre-train enabled  like that pre-trained fine-tuned  Paradigm for for light and deer play  allocation I'm not going to get deep  into the math here but the the gist is  you're basically using the weighted  posterior of your base model as a prior  for uh the model that you want to  fine-tune and this has been implemented  inside Elda which as I said I presented  last year and had some code examples  so I did a simulation study to study  this too to see if the models would  converge in probability to the true  topics  I see them out of time so I'm going to  wrap it up really quickly apologies  but this graphic is a visual of a single  model's posterior converging to the True  Values that were used to generate the  data as you introduce more data and  you're fine-tuning it  uh you know caveat Supply under certain  conditions  um  biggest takeaways are on the left here  but if I can emphasize a couple points  uh this wasn't just about creating a new  tool for LDA uh there's there's Theory  here like I said Zip's law justifies uh  uh using these pre-trained models uh  particularly for for language data  instead of just doing your own  uh and I would I want to move into a  world where we can establish scientific  rigor around the use of language data  so it's in an r package I don't have  time for the novel features nor am I  going to summarize the results but this  is how you can contact me if you'd like
 

